WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.300
Last time, the kidney shed salt until salt out matched salt in, where its line met

2
00:00:05.300 --> 00:00:09.480
the intake. So how does anyone's pressure stay high for years?

3
00:00:13.033 --> 00:00:16.193
Off the crossing, salt keeps draining or piling up.

4
00:00:17.413 --> 00:00:19.773
To hold a new level, the crossing must move.

5
00:00:21.033 --> 00:00:24.753
One way: shift the kidney's line 10 mmHg right.

6
00:00:25.817 --> 00:00:29.017
Now the kidney excretes less sodium at every pressure.

7
00:00:30.217 --> 00:00:34.697
The crossing moves to 110, and holds there, at the same intake.

8
00:00:35.800 --> 00:00:39.820
Another way: tilt the line flatter, pivoting on the old crossing.

9
00:00:41.080 --> 00:00:44.180
At the usual intake, pressure still settles at 100.

10
00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:48.460
These are teaching lines, chosen for easy arithmetic.

11
00:00:49.700 --> 00:00:52.980
Now raise the salt you eat, and the intake line lifts.

12
00:00:54.160 --> 00:00:58.440
Which kidney lets pressure climb further: the normal line, or the flatter one?

13
00:01:04.417 --> 00:01:10.757
The flatter one. The normal line now crosses at 110, a rise of 10.

14
00:01:12.057 --> 00:01:16.017
The flatter line crosses at 120, a rise of 20.

15
00:01:17.100 --> 00:01:22.000
A shallower line means more pressure change per step of salt: salt sensitivity.

16
00:01:23.480 --> 00:01:29.280
A real normal line is much steeper, because angiotensin II and aldosterone fall on a

17
00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:30.280
salty diet.

18
00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:32.500
Here is the one idea.

19
00:01:33.760 --> 00:01:38.080
In Guyton's framework, pressure can rest only where salt out equals salt in.

20
00:01:39.180 --> 00:01:42.760
So a lasting change needs a moved line, or a changed intake.

21
00:01:43.850 --> 00:01:45.170
What moves the line?

22
00:01:46.610 --> 00:01:50.910
Signals that make the kidney hold more sodium at a given pressure push it right.

23
00:01:52.410 --> 00:01:57.090
Angiotensin II, aldosterone and the renal sympathetic nerves do this.

24
00:01:58.410 --> 00:02:00.990
Natriuretic peptides from the heart push it left.

25
00:02:02.050 --> 00:02:08.350
Aldosterone brings sodium channels, called ENaC, to the tubule-facing membrane of

26
00:02:08.350 --> 00:02:09.650
collecting tubule cells.

27
00:02:10.910 --> 00:02:14.690
In Liddle syndrome, mutations leave more of these channels active.

28
00:02:15.850 --> 00:02:18.790
Sodium reabsorption is higher at any pressure.

29
00:02:20.490 --> 00:02:25.690
Volume rises first, then pressure, until salt out matches salt in again, at a higher

30
00:02:25.690 --> 00:02:28.910
level. One channel has moved the line.

31
00:02:29.933 --> 00:02:33.573
Most human hypertension is essential, with many causes.

32
00:02:34.933 --> 00:02:39.073
The working model: the kidney's line is shifted right, flattened, or both.

33
00:02:40.373 --> 00:02:42.633
In people, that is hard to measure directly.

34
00:02:43.817 --> 00:02:47.837
And a moved line tells you where balance lies, not what moved it.

35
00:02:48.917 --> 00:02:52.697
The kidney itself, its nerves, or hormones could each be the cause.

36
00:02:53.833 --> 00:02:57.573
So in this framework, every lasting level is written in salt.

37
00:02:58.853 --> 00:03:03.193
Whatever holds pressure somewhere new works through what the kidney lets go, or what

38
00:03:03.193 --> 00:03:03.753
comes in.
